WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.775
On the sixth of April, twenty twenty-six, a reactor at Kalpakkam started a controlled chain reaction.

2
00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:15.550
It was built to do more than make electricity.

3
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:23.325
It is meant to be a bridge to the reactors of the next generation.

4
00:00:23.325 --> 00:00:31.100
So what is a power station for, if its real job is to prepare the next one?

5
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:58.900
That is the unusual ambition behind India's three-stage nuclear power programme.

6
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.350
The engineering challenge was real.

7
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.800
Nuclear fuel is not a magic stone that produces unlimited power.

8
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.000
Start with a campfire.

9
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.200
A log releases stored chemical energy as it burns.

10
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.400
A nuclear reactor releases energy through controlled changes inside atomic nuclei.

11
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.600
That energy becomes heat.

12
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.800
The heat can produce steam, the steam drives a turbine, and the turbine generates electricity.

13
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.000
The turbine is familiar.

14
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.200
The source of heat is not.

15
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.600
Now picture a farm that does more than harvest crops.

16
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:50.000
It also produces seeds for the next season.

17
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.400
It is only an analogy, but it captures the idea behind a breeder reactor.

18
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.800
The system can convert some material into new usable nuclear fuel while it operates.

19
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:10.933
That does not mean a reactor creates energy from nothing.

20
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:19.067
It means the fuel cycle is designed to make better use of material that would otherwise remain underused.

21
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:27.200
The science, the materials and the safety systems are far more complex than the farming analogy.

22
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.750
India's long-term plan connects different reactor technologies in stages.

23
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:38.300
The early stage established experience with pressurised heavy-water reactors.

24
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.850
The next stage develops fast breeder technology.

25
00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:49.400
The eventual ambition is broader thorium utilisation.

26
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:56.400
India's indigenously designed Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor is rated at five hundred megawatts electric.

27
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:03.400
In April twenty twenty-six it reached first criticality.

28
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:10.400
According to the Department of Atomic Energy, this marked entry into the programme's second stage.

29
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:17.400
First criticality means a controlled chain reaction has been established.

30
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:24.400
It does not mean the reactor has completed every step needed for sustained commercial electricity generation.

31
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.300
Here is a fictional scenario.

32
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:34.200
An energy planner imagines a future fleet of reactors.

33
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.100
Building the first plant is only the beginning.

34
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:44.000
The planner must also account for fuel preparation, maintenance, regulation, waste management and the next generation of facilities.

35
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.900
The challenge is not one machine.

36
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:53.800
It is an entire system across decades.

37
00:03:53.800 --> 00:04:03.800
There is more work ahead.

38
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:13.800
The third stage is still a long-term objective, not a fully deployed national power system.

39
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:23.800
Safe operation, fuel-cycle infrastructure, cost and scale remain essential questions.

40
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:30.100
The achievement deserves respect precisely because the remaining work is difficult.

41
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:36.400
A milestone is not the end of the road.

42
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.700
It tells engineers where the next road begins.
